(p/'E_report.md').write_text('''# Part E report

E.1–E.17 are written in `E.tex`; the register contains 11 Proved, 3 Known and 3 Refuted statements. All external theorem inputs have their primary text opened; the bibliography distinguishes original author PDFs from original text obtained in a reproduction.

The positive special torus group is B3. Its centre maps to -I, and the kernel over SL2(Z) is the square of the centre. The full group is B3 semidirect C2; its orientation-reversing coset inverts the infinite cyclic kernel, so the full GL2 extension is not central. Three literal manuscript normalization formulas are corrected and recorded with exact scans.

The question-mark extension of C1 scan 29 is decided using the later negative kernel convention of the checked transcription of scans 35–36. It is the universal-cover extension with class 1. The opposite-sign pushout also has abstract middle group B3, but is class 5 over the specified quotient and kernel; confusing these two would give an incorrect verdict. Images of C1 scans 20,27,29 were inspected, and the public original PDF was retrieved to inspect scans 35–36 directly. The visible negative fourth power and four lifting maps agree; both sign conventions are fully computed. Overwritten prime labels remain editorially uncertain but are not needed for the proof.

The based normalizer is constructed using actual peripheral-line-preserving automorphisms, with a proved split extension by GT-hat. Its strict-loop-fixed subgroup instead has quotient GT1. The genus-one congruence and characteristic Ferrari-kernel steps are checked against Asada and Minamide–Nakamura, with a direct peripheral centralizer argument. The Tate–Weierstrass arithmetic subgroup is distinguished from the full normalizer. Nakamura’s path cocycle gives the factor a^(-8 rho_2) on the second braid generator and the additional central factor z^(4 rho_2) on the based free-group action.

## Exhaustive finite braid calculation

The completed LINS 0.9/GAP 4.15.1 search enumerates all 1946 normal kernels with B3 quotient order at most 1000: 1000 cyclic and 946 noncyclic. Exactly 471 lie in PB3. The authoritative export is `code/e_b3_quotients_complete.json`; it contains both regular generator permutations for every kernel. The optional SmallGrp label is null where identification data are unavailable, without affecting the permutation certificate or the exhaustive search.

The exact audit checks 255167 noncyclic pairs, finds 100055 two-hexagon solutions and 89721 pairs surjective on H=<a²,b²>; 72251 preserve the target kernel. Every surviving pair records its source kernel, so groupoid arrows are not confused with self-automorphisms. The 471 eligible kernels carry 19147 gentle shadows in the sense of Dolgushev–Guynee. The full pentagon group and gentle group are named separately.

E.15 proves an explicit arity-four construction for every original kernel, including those not contained in PB3. First intersect with PB3, then take K°=(K∩PB3)∩ker(link mod N_ord); the arity-four target is the intersection of four forgetful preimages. All 20 deletion/cabling composites and all 4 pentagon coordinates are checked. Every retained pair lifts to a charming full finite shadow for this declared target, with unchanged commutator associator residue; its source is refined separately. There are 393913 projected charming pairs, including 304192 cyclic ones. This total is indexed by the original marked kernel K; distinct original kernels can induce the same arity-four target, so it is not a count after deduplicating targets. The induced braid quotient has order 6|H-prime|N_ord³; 8 kernels are unchanged, 40 refinements remain ≤1000, and the largest refined order is 5982017994. No giant product group was enumerated to establish these results.

This exhausts the original braid-order bound under the precisely stated projected-shadow convention. It does not enumerate all possible arity-four conductors whose arity-three kernel is exactly the original kernel. Every declared forgetful target kills the Brunnian subgroup; its pentagon can therefore be checked coordinatewise. Genuineness still requires an actual global GT element, and is not inferred from these finite relations.

E.16 determines the full genuine GT, GT0 and G_Q images whenever H has class ≤2: f=[X,Y]^((chi²−1)/24), with the coefficient derived in the torsion-free completed free class-two group before any finite reduction. This gives exact genuine images for 1000 cyclic and 354 noncyclic quotients. The audit certifies 15719 genuine noncyclic pairs in total. The other 592 noncyclic quotients retain exact candidate and source lists, with their complete genuine images unresolved.

The same coefficient proves 180 pairs on 10 eligible kernels are fake: five marked order 486 kernels have 18 candidates and 6 genuine pairs each, and five marked order 972 kernels have 36 candidates and 12 genuine pairs each. `e_fake_shadow.py` gives an independent 54-point permutation certificate for the order 486 example, with H of order 81, central commutator of order 3, X of order 9, and the fake pair m=0,f=[X,Y]. Its finite hexagons and surjectivity are explicit, whereas chi≡1 mod 18 forces the global commutator coefficient to vanish mod 3. A separate arity-four calculation verifies the actual pentagon and computes pure quotient order 3^13 and induced pure arity-three order 3^7, producing a charming fake full shadow on that declared refinement. No novelty claim about these examples is made.

The authors’ published targets N0,N1,N2 were independently reconstructed from their current official PackageGT data. Their pure arity-four orders are 8, 8, 12; induced braid orders 192, 192, 648; practical counts 4, 8, 18; charming counts 4, 4, 6. All charming shadows of N0 and N1 are genuine. These first two targets have the same induced braid kernel but different practical-shadow sets. For N2, the additional four charming shadows beyond identity and complex conjugation are not asserted genuine. The original 13 genuine shadows in the pure-abelian Q3(m) family and the separate matrix diagnostic remain in E.9.

### Reference versions and novelty-search record

Every numbered citation to *What are GT-shadows?* in Part E refers explicitly to arXiv:2008.00066v2 (31 October 2021). The bibliography records its published version in *Algebraic & Geometric Topology*, 24(5), 2721–2777 (2024), DOI 10.2140/agt.2024.24.2721. The published charming definition is 2.20; the preprint definition is 2.19. Every numbered citation to *GT-shadows for the gentle version of the Grothendieck-Teichmueller group* uses arXiv:2401.06870v2 (29 January 2024). Its published record is *Journal of Pure and Applied Algebra*, 229(1), article 107819 (2025), DOI 10.1016/j.jpaa.2024.107819; the publisher record and Crossref DOI metadata were checked on 13 November 2026. Full theorem texts were checked in the stated primary preprints, so publication renumbering is not silently imported.

The literature queries executed on 13 November 2026 included the exact phrases `"GT-shadow" "fake" "486"`, `"charming" "fake" "GT-shadows"`, and `"charming" "fake" "GT" Dolgushev 2025 2026`. The inspected primary material includes the DLL published paper, especially its section 4.2 discussion and Question 4.6; the gentle-paper preprint and publisher introduction; the author research page; and the current official PackageGT documentation and data. The DLL text reports that its authors had no charming fake example at the time of writing. These searches do not establish a priority claim or the absence of later examples. E.16 is presented as an independently verified construction, without claiming that it is the first such example. The two distinct meanings of finite GT-shadow, the source and target kernels, and the global class-two obstruction are stated before any conclusion about fakeness.

Primary URLs: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.00066v2 ; https://msp.org/agt/2024/24-5/agt-v24-n5-p11-p.pdf ; https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.06870v2 ; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2024.107819 ; https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1016/j.jpaa.2024.107819 ; https://sites.temple.edu/vald/research/ ; https://sites.temple.edu/vald/files/2024/05/PackageGT_README.pdf .

### Preserved preliminary and auxiliary runs

`code/e_b3_all_quotients.json` is deliberately retained as a truncated, failed preliminary export; it is not valid complete JSON and must not be used as the quotient table. Its log already completed the 1946-kernel search, but its slower coset-action export was interrupted. `e_b3_quotient_export.g` and `e_b3_quotients_complete.json` supersede it. The optional SmallGrp identification guard avoids the unsupported order 512 identification stage. Checkpoint JSON files from the successful equation audit are also superseded by `e_b3_hexagons.json`.

For Part B, the integral class thirteen q7 certificate is complete in `e_exceptional_lattice_13.json`, with independent full associative-word checks of both genuine group equations. The exact 2708×1336 system, rank 1103 and nullity 233, is preserved in `e_exceptional_matrix_13.json.gz`; its minimum leading multiplier is 1. Large exact minor denominators and Bezout coefficients are in JSON. The older transformed-HNF run `e_exceptional_hnf_13.out` stopped after the rank calculation; it is not a certificate. The separate fixed-class12-word extension attempt stopped under its 180-second alarm after constructing a 1260×630 homogeneous matrix of rank 420, without an obstruction or existence conclusion. It is superseded for existence by the simultaneous class thirteen certificate. These stopped runs do not weaken or extend the completed class thirteen result.


Covering germs of punctured disks are proved to be Z-sets, with the finite part given by Zhat. Tangent rays and paths recover the geometric integer transport torsors; all natural finite-cover transports give their profinite completions. The literal inverse 2-limit of the full small punctured-disk topoi is degenerate: each exterior annulus must vanish, while their union is terminal. The correct covering-germ topos BZ has an explicit small site. Thus the manuscript’s good-case parenthesis cannot be read as an unrestricted assertion about the full topoi in this example, and it has no identification with a Lie approximation tower.

The Homeo/Diff component comparison is stated with the hyperbolicity condition visible on scan 277, and the four low-type exceptions are computed. Radial and logarithmic-spiral homeomorphism germs refute literal equality of the germ groups. A convergent-sequence example with infinitely many sheets refutes uniform descent of arbitrary covering morphisms at an ordinary point; finite coverings and cofinal connected surface disks have the asserted corrected behavior.
''')
